WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:06.540
The first thing I want to talk about in material optimizations is this concept of parent materials.

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:13.839
Now, parent material workflow is not an optimization for performance, but it is a workflow optimization,

3
00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:18.839
as setting your materials up in this manner will allow your team members to work faster,

4
00:00:18.839 --> 00:00:25.339
and also allow you to make massive sweeping changes to the materials in your project with a few clicks of a button.

5
00:00:25.899 --> 00:00:30.899
Material instances work using a parent-child relationship.

6
00:00:30.899 --> 00:00:35.899
So, on the left-hand side of the screen, we have a biological parent setup.

7
00:00:35.899 --> 00:00:41.399
In this very simplified setup of biology, we have parents that pass traits on down to their children.

8
00:00:41.399 --> 00:00:48.899
So, these two parents come together, and out of the red, green, or blue boxes that they have available,

9
00:00:48.899 --> 00:00:54.509
their children will be a red, green, or blue box.

10
00:00:54.509 --> 00:01:02.009
And the reason I'm bringing this up is that, again, in biology, we have parents that have traits that are passed on down to their children,

11
00:01:02.009 --> 00:01:08.010
and their children will select one of those traits, and that's what they'll be presented with.

12
00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:11.010
And this is how our parent material system can work.

13
00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:20.829
Next to this parent-children example, we have two parent materials, a matte ENV building parent and a matte ENV glass parent.

14
00:01:20.829 --> 00:01:25.829
Taking a look at the matte ENV building parent, to the right of it, we have some material instances.

15
00:01:25.829 --> 00:01:32.829
Notice that the first instance, brick 01, looks almost identical to our matte ENV building parent.

16
00:01:32.829 --> 00:01:40.829
The next to that brick 01, we have brick 02. This looks almost identical, but it's a completely different color.

17
00:01:40.829 --> 00:01:49.829
Finally, we have our rock 01 instance, and this looks nothing like either brick 01, brick 02, or even the base parent.

18
00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:55.180
And this really signifies the power of this system.

19
00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:04.140
This means that we can have parent materials that can control massive amounts of props inside of our project,

20
00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:11.139
and when we work inside of them, we can make these changes and have all of those changes propagated down to the children.

21
00:02:11.139 --> 00:02:17.139
Now underneath that, I have this matte ENV glass parent, and notice there's three different ones of those.

22
00:02:17.139 --> 00:02:23.139
Now, the variation inside of these is not as extreme as the above example,

23
00:02:23.139 --> 00:02:32.289
but the reason we have two different versions is because when you're making parent materials,

24
00:02:32.289 --> 00:02:38.289
making one monolithic or massive parent material will not help your project,

25
00:02:38.289 --> 00:02:43.289
and is actually a very inefficient way to work with materials inside of Unreal Engine.

26
00:02:43.289 --> 00:02:51.289
This is because when we build one massive monolithic parent material to try and meet every single need of our materials,

27
00:02:51.289 --> 00:02:58.520
we are going to generate a lot of extra permutations of that material.

28
00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:09.520
Remember, when we're building materials using our material graph, what we are doing is we're actually using a visual interface to write our material.

29
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:20.520
We are not going to be doing this in one draw call, so if we put everything inside of one material that's not going to allow us to save any instructions,

30
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:28.520
that is going to just make that one massive shader extremely inefficient, and also generate a ton of shader permutations,

31
00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:33.930
which is going to blow out our shader cache file, causing long compile times.

32
00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:42.030
What's a better way to approach this is to separate your parent materials into different categories.

33
00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:47.030
For example, if we have environment, we have an environment parent material.

34
00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.030
If we have special effects, we have one for that.

35
00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:54.030
If we need one for glass, we have one for glass, and so on.

36
00:03:54.030 --> 00:04:02.030
This way we have a very versatile material, but one that is not set up to meet every need,

37
00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:06.030
but to meet many needs of a select group of assets.

38
00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:11.030
There is no limit on the amount of parent materials you can have, or material instances,

39
00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:15.030
but you should take some time to consider how you're going to set things up,

40
00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:22.029
so that when you are utilizing parent materials and material instances in your project,

41
00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:27.029
artists and designers will understand which materials they should be using to base their work off of.
